  <data name="Warning_KeyWorldReadable_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Private key is readable by others</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_KeyWorldReadable_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>OpenSSH refuses to use a private key that other accounts on this machine can read. Set the file mode to 0600 so that only you can read it.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_RsaTooShort_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>RSA key is shorter than 3072 bits</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_RsaTooShort_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Short RSA keys no longer meet current guidance. Generate a new Ed25519 key, or an RSA key of at least 3072 bits, and replace this one on every host that trusts it.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_DsaDeprecated_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>DSA key found</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_DsaDeprecated_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>DSA is disabled by default in current OpenSSH releases and will stop working. Replace it with an Ed25519 key.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_KeyNoPassphrase_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Private key has no passphrase</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_KeyNoPassphrase_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Anyone who copies this file gets your access. Add a passphrase and load the key into an agent so you still type it only once per session.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_OrphanedPublicKey_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Public key without a private key</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_OrphanedPublicKey_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A .pub file was found with no matching private key. Either the private key lives elsewhere, or it is held only by a hardware token or an agent.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_PlaintextCredentialStore_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Credentials stored in plain text</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_PlaintextCredentialStore_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This store keeps passwords and tokens unencrypted on disk. Switch the credential helper to your operating system&apos;s vault.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_MissingPublicKey_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No .pub file next to the private key</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_MissingPublicKey_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Several tools expect the public key to sit beside the private one. GitVault can write it: for an unencrypted key it derives it directly, and for a protected key it asks for the passphrase first.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_KeyIntegrityFailed_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The key file failed its own integrity check</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_KeyIntegrityFailed_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A PuTTY key file stores a MAC over its own contents, and this one does not match. The file has been edited or damaged since it was written; do not rely on it until you have checked where it came from.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_AgentNotRunning_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>SSH agent is not running</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_AgentNotRunning_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Keys cannot be held in memory while the agent is stopped, so you will be asked for the passphrase on every connection.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_GitNotFound_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No git executable found</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_GitNotFound_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>GitVault fell back to reading the configuration files itself. Conditional includes and platform path quirks are resolved less accurately than git would resolve them. Install git, or add it to PATH.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_NoIdentityConfigured_Title" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>No author identity configured</value>
  </data>
  <data name="Warning_NoIdentityConfigured_Body" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Neither user.name nor user.email is set anywhere, so git will refuse to create a commit. Set them globally, or per repository through a profile.</value>
  </data>
